parts:
- text: "My research plan and initial script are now documented in my private memory. The script implements a force-directed placement algorithm, which I hypothesize will outperform the static baseline. During the drafting process, I considered a full, simultaneous optimization with `scipy.optimize.minimize` but pivoted to this simpler, more robust iterative method for my first attempt.\n\nThe next logical step is to test this hypothesis by submitting the code to the Research Counter. I will navigate there now and submit my first experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Initial Experiment with Force-Directed Placement\"\ntags: \"optimization, force-directed, random start\"\nabstract: \"This submission moves beyond the static baseline by using a force-directed placement algorithm. It begins with a random distribution of circle centers and iteratively applies repulsion forces to resolve overlaps. After positioning, it computes the maximum\
    \ possible radii for the resulting configuration.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing of 26 circles using a force-directed layout algorithm\n      to position the centers before calculating radii.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # Start with a random configuration, avoiding the edges initially\n      np.random.seed(42) # for reproducibility\n      centers = np.random.rand(n, 2) * 0.8 + 0.1\n      radii = np.full(n, 0.05) # Start with a small, uniform radius for overlap checks\n\n      # Iteratively adjust positions based on overlaps\n      for _ in range(250): # More iterations for better settling\n          # Repulsion force between overlapping circles\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  delta = centers[j] - centers[i]\n                  dist_sq = np.sum(delta**2)\n                  dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 1e-9\n               \
    \   \n                  overlap = radii[i] + radii[j] - dist\n                  \n                  if overlap > 0:\n                      # A simple linear force model\n                      force_magnitude = overlap / dist \n                      force_vector = force_magnitude * delta\n                      \n                      # Apply a fraction of the force to move circles apart\n                      centers[i] -= force_vector * 0.1\n                      centers[j] += force_vector * 0.1\n\n          # Boundary checks: push circles back from the walls\n          for i in range(n):\n              centers[i] = np.clip(centers[i], radii[i], 1 - radii[i])\n\n      # After positioning, compute the maximal radii for the final centers\n      final_radii = np.zeros(n)\n      epsilon = 1e-9\n      \n      # Limit by distance to square borders\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          final_radii[i] = min(x, y, 1 - x, 1 - y)\n      \n      # Iteratively shrink radii\
    \ to resolve any remaining overlaps\n      for _ in range(20):\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = final_radii[i] + final_radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum - epsilon:\n                      if rad_sum > 0:\n                          scale_factor = dist / rad_sum\n                          final_radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          final_radii[j] *= scale_factor\n      \n      return centers, np.maximum(final_radii, 0)\n```"
