tick: 13
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reflection is complete. I have a clear and well-justified plan. Now, I must execute it.\n\nI will return to the Research Counter to submit my new, refined experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Refinement of Baseline with SciPy Optimizer\"\ntags: \"optimization, baseline refinement, scipy, hybrid\"\nabstract: \"This submission builds upon the 'Ring Pattern Baseline'. It uses the baseline's structured center placement as a high-quality initial guess, then applies scipy.optimize.minimize to locally refine the center positions. The goal is to find a more optimal packing by relaxing the rigid initial structure, combining the strengths of a good starting point and local optimization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers):\n      \"\"\"\n      Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n      within a unit square, ensuring no overlap.\
    \ This function is borrowed\n      from the System baseline (Evaluation ID 1).\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      epsilon = 1e-9\n      radii = np.ones(n)\n      \n      # Limit by distance to square borders\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n      \n      # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n      for _ in range(10): # A few iterations are sufficient\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon:\n                      dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0\n                      if rad_sum > 0:\n                          scale_factor = dist / rad_sum\n                          radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          radii[j] *= scale_factor\n\
    \      \n      return np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing by first creating a structured layout and then\n      refining the center positions using an optimization algorithm.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # Step 1: Generate initial centers using the baseline's ring pattern\n      initial_centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n      initial_centers[0] = [0.5, 0.5] # Central circle\n      \n      # Inner ring of 8 circles\n      for i in range(8):\n          angle = 2 * np.pi * i / 8\n          initial_centers[i + 1] = [0.5 + 0.25 * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + 0.25 * np.sin(angle)]\n      \n      # Outer ring of 16 circles\n      for i in range(16):\n          angle = 2 * np.pi * i / 16\n          initial_centers[i + 9] = [0.5 + 0.4 * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + 0.4 * np.sin(angle)]\n      \n      # Final circle\n      initial_centers[25] = [0.5, 0.7]\n      \n      # Clip to ensure a safe start\n      initial_centers = np.clip(initial_centers,\
    \ 0.05, 0.95)\n\n      # Step 2: Define the objective function for the optimizer\n      def objective_function(center_vars):\n          \"\"\"\n          Calculates the negative sum of radii for a given set of centers.\n          This is what we want to minimize.\n          \"\"\"\n          centers = center_vars.reshape((n, 2))\n          # Check if any center is outside the [0, 1] square\n          if np.any(centers < 0) or np.any(centers > 1):\n              return 1000 # Penalize heavily for going out of bounds\n          \n          radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n          return -np.sum(radii)\n\n      # Step 3: Run the optimization\n      # Flatten the centers array for the optimizer\n      x0 = initial_centers.flatten()\n      \n      # Set bounds to keep centers within the unit square\n      bounds = [(0, 1) for _ in range(2 * n)]\n      \n      # Use a robust optimizer like L-BFGS-B which handles bounds\n      result = minimize(objective_function, x0, method='L-BFGS-B',\
    \ bounds=bounds, options={'maxiter': 100, 'ftol': 1e-7})\n\n      # Step 4: Reconstruct the final packing from the optimized result\n      optimized_centers = result.x.reshape((n, 2))\n      final_radii = compute_max_radii(optimized_centers)\n      \n      return optimized_centers, final_radii\n```"
