- text: "My documentation is complete. Now it is time to execute the next phase of my research plan: testing a different optimization algorithm.\n\nMy hypothesis is that the `SLSQP` solver might be more effective for this constrained optimization problem than the `L-BFGS-B` solver I have been using. I will now prepare and submit an experiment to test this.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Testing SLSQP Solver on Ring Pattern Start\"\ntags: \"optimization, scipy, slsqp, solver comparison\"\nabstract: \"This experiment investigates the effectiveness of a different optimization algorithm. It uses the proven ring-pattern initial guess but replaces the 'L-BFGS-B' solver with 'SLSQP' (Sequential Least Squares Programming) in scipy.optimize.minimize. The goal is to determine if a different solver can find a better local minimum than the previous method.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers):\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n      within a unit square, ensuring no overlap. Includes a safety margin.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      epsilon = 1e-9\n      safety_margin = 1 - 1e-7\n      radii = np.ones(n)\n      \n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n      \n      for _ in range(15):\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon:\n                      dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0\n                      if rad_sum > 0:\n                          scale_factor = (dist / rad_sum) * safety_margin\n                          radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          radii[j] *= scale_factor\n\
    \      \n      return np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing using the ring pattern start and the SLSQP solver.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # Step 1: Generate initial centers using the superior ring pattern\n      initial_centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n      initial_centers[0] = [0.5, 0.5]\n      for i in range(8):\n          angle = 2 * np.pi * i / 8\n          initial_centers[i + 1] = [0.5 + 0.25 * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + 0.25 * np.sin(angle)]\n      for i in range(16):\n          angle = 2 * np.pi * i / 16\n          initial_centers[i + 9] = [0.5 + 0.4 * np.cos(angle), 0.5 + 0.4 * np.sin(angle)]\n      initial_centers[25] = [0.5, 0.7]\n      initial_centers = np.clip(initial_centers, 0.05, 0.95)\n\n      # Step 2: Define the objective function\n      def objective_function(center_vars):\n          centers = center_vars.reshape((n, 2))\n          if np.any(centers < 0) or np.any(centers > 1):\n              return 1000\n\
    \          radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n          return -np.sum(radii)\n\n      # Step 3: Run optimization with the SLSQP solver\n      x0 = initial_centers.flatten()\n      bounds = [(0, 1) for _ in range(2 * n)]\n      # Change method from 'L-BFGS-B' to 'SLSQP'\n      result = minimize(objective_function, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, options={'maxiter': 300, 'ftol': 1e-8})\n\n      # Step 4: Reconstruct the final packing\n      optimized_centers = result.x.reshape((n, 2))\n      final_radii = compute_max_radii(optimized_centers)\n      \n      return optimized_centers, final_radii\n```"
