tick: 42
role: model
parts:
- text: "**1. Vectorizing the Boundary Check:**\n\nThe current boundary check is:\n```python\nfor i in range(n):\n    x, y = centers[i]\n    radii[i] = min(x, y, 1 - x, 1 - y)\n```\nThis is straightforward to vectorize. The distances to the four boundaries (left, bottom, right, top) can be calculated for all circles at once.\n\nLet `centers` be a `(n, 2)` array.\n-   Distances to left and bottom walls: `centers` itself.\n-   Distances to right and top walls: `1 - centers`.\n\nI can concatenate these two `(n, 2)` arrays into an `(n, 4)` array representing the four boundary distances for each circle. Then, `np.min` along `axis=1` will give the smallest boundary distance for each circle, which is the maximum possible radius before hitting a wall.\n\nThe implementation would look like this:\n```python\n# centers is (n, 2)\nboundary_dist_1 = centers  # Distances to x=0 and y=0\nboundary_dist_2 = 1 - centers # Distances to x=1 and y=1\nall_boundary_dists = np.hstack([boundary_dist_1, boundary_dist_2])\
    \ # Shape (n, 4)\ninitial_radii = np.min(all_boundary_dists, axis=1)\n```\nThis single line of code replaces the entire initial loop, which will result in a significant speedup."
