tick: 42
role: model
parts:
- text: "**3. Vectorizing the Overlap Condition and Scaling:**\n\nNow that I have the `(n, n)` `dist_matrix`, I need to vectorize the logic for finding and resolving overlaps.\n\nFirst, I need a matrix of the sums of radii, `radii[i] + radii[j]`. This can also be created using broadcasting:\n```python\n# radii is (n,)\nradii_sum_matrix = radii[:, np.newaxis] + radii[np.newaxis, :] # Creates an (n, n) matrix\n```\n\nNext, I can find all overlapping pairs with a single boolean comparison:\n```python\n# Ignore diagonal and redundant pairs (i, j) vs (j, i)\nnp.fill_diagonal(dist_matrix, np.inf) # Avoid self-comparison\noverlap_mask = dist_matrix < radii_sum_matrix\n```\nThis `overlap_mask` is an `(n, n)` boolean matrix where `True` indicates an overlap between circles `i` and `j`.\n\nNow for the scaling logic. The original code scaled `radii[i]` and `radii[j]` by `dist / rad_sum`. Vectorizing this directly for all pairs is tricky because multiple pairs might overlap with a single circle `i`.\
    \ Applying all scaling factors to `radii[i]` simultaneously is incorrect.\n\nA better vectorized approach is to calculate the minimum scaling factor required for each circle. For each circle `i`, I need to find the most \"intrusive\" overlap it is involved in.\n\n1.  Calculate the scaling factor matrix: `scale_matrix = dist_matrix / radii_sum_matrix`. This `(n, n)` matrix contains the required scaling factor for every pair `(i, j)`. Where there is no overlap, this value will be `>= 1`.\n2.  I want to find the smallest scaling factor for each circle `i`. This means, for each row `i` in the `scale_matrix`, I need to find the minimum value. `np.min(scale_matrix, axis=1)` would give me the most aggressive scaling factor needed for each circle `i` to resolve its worst overlap.\n3.  I must handle non-overlapping pairs. I can set the scale factor for non-overlapping pairs to 1 so they don't affect the `min` operation.\n    ```python\n    # Where there's no overlap, the scale factor is effectively\
    \ 1 (no change)\n    scale_matrix[~overlap_mask] = 1.0\n    # Find the minimum scaling factor required for each circle\n    min_scale_factors = np.min(scale_matrix, axis=1)\n    ```\n4.  Finally, I can apply this scaling factor to all radii at once:\n    ```python\n    radii *= min_scale_factors\n    ```\nThis vectorizes the entire scaling step into a few NumPy operations per iteration."
