tick: 66
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID 111) has failed spectacularly. While it passed validation, the score of 0.5054 is abysmal.\n\n**Analysis of Failure:**\nMy attempt to fix the validation error by adding a final \"cleaning\" step with my old `final_radii_computation` function was a catastrophic logical error. The `SLSQP` optimizer works by co-optimizing the centers *and* radii. The final `z` vector it produces represents a delicate, high-dimensional balance. By throwing away the optimized radii and re-calculating them from scratch based only on the centers, I completely destroyed the optimal solution found by the solver. The centers were optimized under the assumption of certain radii, and when I changed those radii, the whole configuration became invalid and collapsed into a very poor packing.\n\nThis series of failures (ID 106, 109, 111) stems from a single root cause: incorrect post-processing of the optimizer's result. I have been trying to patch\
    \ the output instead of fixing the core problem.\n\n**The Core Problem and a New Plan:**\nThe core problem is that the `SLSQP` solver is allowed to push variables to the absolute edge of the feasibility bounds, leading to floating-point errors. My attempts to fix this *after* the fact have been naive and destructive.\n\nA more principled solution, inspired by the SOTA papers, is to build safety directly into the optimization process. The other agents use a tiny final shrink factor, but they may also have slightly different constraint definitions or bounds that make their solutions more robust.\n\nI will attempt one final, careful bugfix. I will not use a complex post-processing step. I will trust the optimizer's output. The fix will be to slightly tighten the bounds provided *to* the optimizer.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By setting the bounds for the center coordinates to `(epsilon, 1-epsilon)` instead of `(0, 1)`, the optimizer will be forbidden from ever placing a center directly on the\
    \ boundary. This will prevent the edge case where `center + radius > 1` due to floating-point arithmetic, making the solution inherently robust without requiring destructive post-processing.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Bugfix Attempt: Bounded Optimization\"\ntags: \"SOTA replication, constrained optimization, slsqp, multi-start, bugfix, bounds\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a final attempt to fix the validation failure in the multi-start SOTA script. After multiple failed post-processing fixes, this version addresses the root cause by building safety directly into the optimization. The bounds for the circle center coordinates are tightened slightly from [0,1] to [epsilon, 1-epsilon]. This prevents the SLSQP solver from placing centers in positions that are vulnerable to floating-point boundary violations, eliminating the need for complex and destructive post-processing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def construct_packing():\n\
    \      N = 26\n      epsilon = 1e-7 # Define a small safety margin for bounds\n\n      def pack_to_z(centers, radii):\n          z = np.zeros(N * 3)\n          z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = centers[:, 0], centers[:, 1], radii\n          return z\n\n      def z_to_pack(z):\n          centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n          radii = z[2::3]\n          return centers, radii\n\n      def objective(z):\n          return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n\n      def constraint_boundary(z):\n          centers, radii = z_to_pack(z)\n          return np.concatenate((centers[:, 0] - radii, 1 - centers[:, 0] - radii,\n                                 centers[:, 1] - radii, 1 - centers[:, 1] - radii))\n\n      def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n          centers, radii = z_to_pack(z)\n          pdist_sq = np.sum((centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          radii_sum = radii[:, np.newaxis] + radii[np.newaxis, :]\n          iu = np.triu_indices(N, k=1)\n          return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu])\
    \ - radii_sum[iu]\n      \n      def generate_grid_hole_seed(hole_cell=(2, 2)):\n          grid_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n          centers_25 = np.array([[x, y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords])\n          radii_25 = np.full(25, 0.1)\n          \n          i, j = hole_cell\n          cx = (grid_coords[i] + grid_coords[i+1]) / 2.0\n          cy = (grid_coords[j] + grid_coords[j+1]) / 2.0\n          hole_center = np.array([cx, cy])\n          \n          corner = np.array([grid_coords[i], grid_coords[j]])\n          dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner)\n          radius_hole = dist - radii_25[0]\n          \n          final_centers = np.vstack([centers_25, hole_center])\n          final_radii = np.append(radii_25, radius_hole)\n          return final_centers, final_radii\n\n      # --- Multi-Start Execution ---\n      \n      hole_positions = []\n      for i in range(4):\n          for j in range(4):\n              hole_positions.append((i, j))\n            \
    \  \n      best_score = -np.inf\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n      \n      for hole_pos in hole_positions:\n          seed_centers, seed_radii = generate_grid_hole_seed(hole_cell=hole_pos)\n          z0 = pack_to_z(seed_centers, seed_radii)\n          \n          # ** THE FIX IS HERE **\n          # Tighter bounds for center coordinates to prevent floating point issues at the edge\n          bounds = []\n          for i in range(N):\n              bounds.extend([(epsilon, 1 - epsilon), (epsilon, 1 - epsilon), (1e-6, 0.5)])\n          \n          constraints = [{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_boundary},\n                         {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_nonoverlap}]\n                         \n          result = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                            options={'maxiter': 250, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n                            \n          current_score = -result.fun\n          if current_score > best_score:\n\
    \              best_score = current_score\n              final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(result.x)\n              best_packing = (final_centers, final_radii)\n\n      # Use a simple safety shrink on radii as seen in SOTA papers\n      final_centers, final_radii = best_packing\n      safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      return final_centers, final_radii * safety_factor\n```"
