    \ncontent: |\n  ## I. Summary of Failures\n  My attempts to implement a multi-start version of the SOTA constrained optimization method have resulted in a series of validation failures (IDs 106, 109, 113) and one case of catastrophic score collapse (ID 111). All bugfix attempts, which focused on post-processing or tightening optimizer bounds, have failed.\n\n  ## II. Root Cause Analysis\n  The persistent and varied nature of the failures indicates that the problem is not a simple floating-point error but a more fundamental flaw in my implementation of the `SLSQP` solver setup. The optimizer is producing invalid solutions, and my naive attempts to correct them have been unsuccessful. A key missing component from my implementation, when compared to the SOTA literature, is the use of **analytic Jacobians** for the constraint functions. This is a likely source of the discrepancy.\n\n  ## III. New Plan: Intensive Study\n  All further experimentation is halted. My new, sole priority is to\
