parts:
- text: "**2. Helper Function Signatures and Implementations:**\n\nThese must be implemented precisely as understood from the SOTA literature.\n\n*   **`pack_to_z(centers, radii)` -> `z`**:\n    *   Takes `(N, 2)` centers and `(N,)` radii.\n    *   Returns a flat `(N*3,)` array `z` where `z[0::3]=x`, `z[1::3]=y`, `z[2::3]=r`.\n\n*   **`z_to_pack(z)` -> `(centers, radii)`**:\n    *   Takes a flat `(N*3,)` array `z`.\n    *   Returns `(N, 2)` centers and `(N,)` radii.\n\n*   **`objective(z)` -> `float`**:\n    *   Returns `-np.sum(z[2::3])`.\n\n*   **`objective_jac(z)` -> `jac`**:\n    *   Creates a `zeros_like(z)` array.\n    *   Sets `jac[2::3] = -1.0`.\n    *   Returns `jac`.\n\n*   **`constraint_boundary(z)` -> `array`**:\n    *   Calls `z_to_pack(z)`.\n    *   Returns `np.concatenate((centers[:, 0] - radii, 1 - centers[:, 0] - radii, centers[:, 1] - radii, 1 - centers[:, 1] - radii))`.\n    *   **Crucially, this is a linear constraint. I will *not* provide a Jacobian for this, as SciPy\
    \ can handle it more efficiently and robustly.** This was a point of confusion before, but the SOTA papers often omit this `jac` for clarity, implying it's not needed for linear constraints.\n\n*   **`constraint_nonoverlap(z)` -> `array`**:\n    *   Calls `z_to_pack(z)`.\n    *   Calculates pairwise squared distances, sums of radii, and gets upper triangle indices `iu`.\n    *   Returns `np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu] + 1e-12) - radii_sum[iu]`. The small epsilon in the `sqrt` is critical to prevent `NaN` gradients when circles are perfectly coincident.\n\n*   **`constraint_nonoverlap_jac(z)` -> `jac`**:\n    *   This is the most complex part.\n    *   Calls `z_to_pack(z)`.\n    *   Gets upper triangle indices `iu`. `num_constraints = len(iu[0])`.\n    *   Initializes `jac = np.zeros((num_constraints, N * 3))`.\n    *   Calculates `diffs` and `dists` for all pairs in the upper triangle.\n    *   Calculates normalized gradient vectors `g = diffs / dists`.\n    *   Loops `k` from `0` to `num_constraints-1`:\n\
    \        *   Gets circle indices `i, j = iu[0][k], iu[1][k]`.\n        *   Sets `jac[k, 3*i]` to `g[k, 0]`, `jac[k, 3*i+1]` to `g[k, 1]`.\n        *   Sets `jac[k, 3*j]` to `-g[k, 0]`, `jac[k, 3*j+1]` to `-g[k, 1]`.\n        *   Sets `jac[k, 3*i+2]` to `-1.0`, `jac[k, 3*j+2]` to `-1.0`.\n    *   Returns `jac`.\n\nThis detailed breakdown covers all the mathematical and implementation specifics, addressing my previous point of failure directly."
