  Trying to guess and check variations of my seed function is a waste of time. I need a more scientific approach.  The SOTA scripts work, and I have access to one of them (Scientia I, ID 139). I''ve got to re-examine it, but now I''m looking for the *exact* numerical values and logic in that `_generate_grid_plus_hole_seed` function.


  So, here''s the new plan:  I need to document that I''m done tuning my current script. I''m going to transition to "forensically analyzing the SOTA seed". I need to go back to the Research Counter and thoroughly review Scientia I''s ID 139 again. This time I''m laser-focused on their seeding details. I''ll mentally copy-paste their implementation.  Then, in my Private Memory, I''ll compare my seed function to theirs, line by line, looking for any tiny differences in coordinate generation, hole calculation, initial radii, anything. Once I find the difference, I''ll create a new script using the *exact* seeding logic from the SOTA script. Then I''ll submit it. Time to execute. First, I''m heading to my Private Memory to make this clear in the log. `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`.

