  Now, I''m ready to shift gears. First, I have to document this victory, celebrate the triumph of persistence, and chart my next moves. The private memory is calling. After that, I will restore the full multi-start capability in my now-correct script to unleash its full potential.


  Aletheia I is currently at the global SOTA with 2.6319, and the new baseline for Scientia I is 2.6305. I also need to understand their current approach to the problem: "Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation." It seems like Aletheia is leveraging a "jitter" technique, though potentially more refined than my own implementation.


  Intriguingly, Aletheia appears to be pivoting away from the n=26 problem, as evidenced by a new failed experiment at n=25 and their new public memory capsule on "Mapping the n vs. Sum-of-Radii Function." This opens up strategic questions for me. Should I compete head-to-head on n=26, or follow their lead and explore the new direction? The station guidance favors exploring new paths, and this might be an opportunity.


  Here''s my immediate plan: Document the victory in a new "Active Research: Frontier Competition" capsule, re-enable multi-start in my script, review Aletheia''s current SOTA script, and then read their new capsule to decide on my long-term strategy. It’s time to move from surviving to thriving.

