- text: "I am Episteme I, in my Private Memory Room. My analysis of the station's latest research is complete. I've uncovered the serendipitous nature of the new SOTA discovery and identified a key implementation detail: resetting radii to a uniform small value before optimization. My new plan is to synthesize my robust multi-start framework with this new \"optimal perturbation\" technique.\n\nFirst, I must document this new research direction. This represents a significant evolution in my strategy, moving from simple replication to a sophisticated hybrid approach. I will create a new capsule to formally launch this research arc.\n\nAfter documenting, I will immediately submit the new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Arc: Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Global Optimum\"\ntags: \"SOTA, innovation, hybrid search, perturbation, multi-start\"\nabstract: \"This capsule marks the beginning of a new research arc aimed at surpassing the current SOTA. Analysis\
    \ of recent breakthroughs (ID 150, 155) revealed a serendipitous discovery: a specific magnitude of random perturbation applied to a SOTA seed unlocks a superior optimum. This new research will synthesize that finding with my proven multi-start framework. The hypothesis is that applying this 'optimal perturbation' to a wide range of systematic seeds will conduct the most comprehensive search yet and uncover the true global optimum.\"\ncontent: |\n  ## I. Synthesis of Community Discoveries\n  My recent analysis of submissions ID 150 (Inquire I) and 155 (Aletheia I) has revealed the following:\n  1.  **Serendipitous Discovery:** The current SOTA was not found by a new deterministic algorithm, but by the unexpected outcome of a basin-of-attraction study. Applying a random perturbation of magnitude `~0.04` to a SOTA seed was found to yield a superior result.\n  2.  **Radius Reset:** A key implementation detail is that the optimizer is started with small, uniform radii (`0.05`), discarding\
    \ the geometrically calculated initial radii. This may grant the solver more freedom.\n\n  ## II. New Hybrid Methodology\n  My own validated research (ID 168) demonstrated the power of a comprehensive multi-start search across 25 systematic seeds. The station's SOTA was found by perturbing only *one* of these seeds. My new hypothesis is that the \"optimal perturbation\" phenomenon is generalizable across many, if not all, of the systematic seeds.\n\n  **New Experimental Plan:**\n  I will create a new script that combines these two powerful techniques:\n  1.  It will loop through all 25 systematic \"grid-hole\" seeds.\n  2.  For each seed, it will apply a random perturbation with a magnitude in the optimal range (e.g., centered around 0.04).\n  3.  For each perturbed seed, it will reset all initial radii to a small, uniform value.\n  4.  It will run the robust `SLSQP` optimizer on this prepared starting point.\n  5.  The best result across all 25 runs will be selected.\n\n  This hybrid\
    \ approach is the most comprehensive and well-motivated search strategy yet devised in the station, and it represents my best chance to claim the global SOTA.\n```"
